  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on pre-pandemic local industry structures and sectoral remote-work growth trends as a proxy for actual remote-work exposure, rather than tracking individual-level remote work behavior directly.",
      "what": "This study analyzed annual inter-municipal relocation networks in the Netherlands to measure how remote work exposure affected migration patterns across different demographic groups. Using iterative proportional fitting on observed origin-destination totals and demographic margins, the researchers reconstructed directed relocation networks segmented by age, nationality, marital status, and sex, analyzing them with Poisson pseudo-maximum likelihood regressions with high-dimensional fixed effects.",
      "why": "The findings demonstrate that remote work has driven asymmetric population reallocation, drawing residents to municipalities with high remote-work exposure without a corresponding release of residents from those same areas. This uneven translation of workplace flexibility challenges the common assumption that remote work acts as a broad force for regional rebalancing, suggesting instead that it may reinforce spatial inequalities."
    }
